window.docContents[210].resolve({"/ISO___IEC-80000-___-The-Standards-Catalogue/The-references-layer-_LPAR_citation-metadata-only_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO___IEC-80000-___-The-Standards-Catalogue--The-references-layer-_LPAR_citation-metadata-only_RPAR_":{"contents":"\n\nA standard reference StandardRef is the bibliographic identity of one part: its\npublishing body (ISO or IEC), the series number (), the part number, the\nedition designation as printed, the publication date, and the part's English title.\nIt carries no normative content — only what is needed to cite the source. Its\ncite renders the conventional form, e.g. IEC 80000-6, Edition 2.0, 2022-11.\n\nRealized as structure StandardRef (Mathlib-free) with a StandardBody\n(ISO/IEC) field and the printed edition, date, and title as strings, plus\ndesignation (ISO 80000-3) and cite (the full reference). The values are read\noff each licensed part's cover page; nothing normative is transcribed.\n\nThe catalogue is the list of the thirteen catalogued parts in part order, each a\nstandard reference. It is the single place this work\nnames which editions of ISO/IEC 80000 the catalogued quantity-kinds and units are\ngrounded against, so the grounding is auditable rather than implicit.\n\nRealized as catalogue : List StandardRef, the thirteen definitions iso80000_1 …\niec80000_13 (with iec80000_6 and iec80000_13 the IEC-published parts). The\nedition is \"Second edition\" for every part except those two, both \"Edition 2.0\"; the\ndates are the cover dates of the licensed copies.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009ISO/IEC 80000 — The Standards Catalogue","header":"21.1. The references layer (citation metadata only)","id":"/ISO___IEC-80000-___-The-Standards-Catalogue/The-references-layer-_LPAR_citation-metadata-only_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO___IEC-80000-___-The-Standards-Catalogue--The-references-layer-_LPAR_citation-metadata-only_RPAR_"},"/Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework/What-the-calculus-built-on-the-framework/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework--What-the-calculus-built-on-the-framework":{"contents":"The library PropertyKindCalculus.Torch is where the calculus meets TorchLean. Part of it is\nmetrology, and part of it is framework machinery that any consumer of a TorchLean tape could\nuse.\n\n sets the two side by side, layer by layer. The layers fall into\ntwo classes.\n\nTwo layers have no counterpart in TorchLean. Meaning: a TorchLean tensor holds numbers, and\nthe kinds, the provenance hypergraph and the seal are the calculus's own. Performance: the\narithmetic intensity of a recorded model, the peak residency of its live intermediates under\neach strategy, and the parallelism a confined platform can sustain are computed by the calculus\nfrom the same recording that carries the faithfulness proofs. A search of TorchLean's source on\nthe pinned revision for intensity, roofline, residency, liveness and platform-capacity terms\nfinds nothing; that is a measurement of the source text, and it is stated as one.\n\nThe performance layer reaches the CPU as well as the GPU. Because a model is branch-free and\nits pixels independent, the whole model runs on contiguous slices of a tile in parallel. The\nnumber of slices is the largest that pays off: no more than the cores can run or free memory can\nhold, and, where a program's per-task cost has been measured, no more than the count that\nminimizes its modeled run time, past which each extra slice costs more to start than it saves. On a workstation with a 28-core\nIntel Xeon w7-3465X, the soil-moisture deployment's four tile programs (the parameter fit and\nthree retrievals) run 12.6 to 24.0 times faster than on one core: the median of three runs of\ncompute time, each against the same program's own one-slice run, over tiles of 0.41 to 6.55\nmillion pixels (BENCHMARKS-A4500-56cores.md, section 4, in the soil-moisture-workflows\nrepository). Those figures are measurements. What is proved about that path is the kinds of the\nsizing arithmetic, which keep a count of slices from being confused with a count of bytes; that\nthe slices together compute what one run over the whole tile computes rests on the independence\nof the pixels, and is not proved: no theorem states it, and no checked-in test compares the two\nruns bit for bit.\n\nAt the other five layers the calculus builds on what TorchLean provides. Uncertainty:\nTorchLean bounds round-off; the calculus states measurement uncertainty along the GUM, Willink\nand SSPRC methods, with coverage intervals and conformity assessment, and charges round-off to\nthat budget as numerical adequacy, written in TorchLean's binary32 rounding vocabulary\n(the uncertainty chapter). Code generation: TorchLean compiles one scalar\nfunction to one kernel; the calculus compiles a whole model to one, and prints the same program\nas C and Julia, where TorchLean reaches Julia only as an external process. Kernel alphabet:\nthe addition is the operations remote-sensing forward models are built from: attenuation and\nextinction are exponentials, dielectric mixing takes square roots and logarithms, branch-free\nselection takes min and max, and calibration curves are tables. Recording and Numbers:\nthe recording carrier and the carrier ladder are built on TorchLean's tape and floating-point\nformats. TorchLean's tape records each operation it is given; the sharing recorder adds a tape\nthat is proved to be the compaction of that recording, at the memory of the model's graph\ninstead of its expression tree.\n\nWhat PropertyKindCalculus adds beyond TorchLean, layer by layer.The left column is TorchLean; a #n marks a change contributed to it by this work, and a dashed\nbox a layer where TorchLean's source has no counterpart. The right column is what the calculus\nadds at that layer. In the kernel alphabet, the filled chips are\nthe operations TorchLean's kernel compiler Program.of refuses in binary32 and binary64, on the\nrevision the calculus pins; TorchLean's eager device and tape operations include exp, log and\nsqrt, so the extension is to one certified fused kernel holding a model's whole arithmetic.\nThe table fetch is shared: its device kernel is a TorchLean Program.of kernel, and its\nsemantics is the calculus's vocabulary extension.\nThe last two rows of chips on the left are eager device-buffer operations contributed by this\nwork (#52, #53): LibTorch calls on buffers, not operations of a certified Program.of kernel.\n\n* Modules* What they provide* Reading\n* TapeM, TapeFaithful* the elementwise recorders over TorchLean's single entry Tape.apply, and their forward-value lemmas for symbolic inputs* framework machinery: depends on TorchLean alone\n* TapeCse, with its denotation theorems* value-preserving common-subexpression elimination over a recorded tape, keyed on operation, remapped parents and value bits* framework machinery\n* TapeSharedCarrier, with shares_record* a recording carrier that shares sub-expressions while it records, proved to emit the compaction of the unshared recording* framework machinery, keyed on the calculus's NumCarrier\n* TapeEval, TapeStraightLine, TapeCodegen, TapeCodegenJulia* the tape's forward denotation; one straight-line program per tape with one denotation theorem; CUDA, C and Julia printers of it; arithmetic-intensity and peak-residency accounting* framework machinery, over an alphabet that includes exp, log, sqrt, min, max and the table fetch\n* CudaCarrier, BatchCarrier* the branchless carrier on device buffers; the backend boundary as a class, and task-sharded CPU execution* framework machinery, keyed on the calculus's NumCarrier\n* Fp32Spec, Fp32, Fp32Math* the binary32 rungs of the carrier ladder, with the executable rung's side condition, and the record that the executable carrier has a smaller function vocabulary than ℝ and none of its function laws* metrology\n* TapeCarrier, TapeParity, TapeHom, TapeSeal* the recording carrier, the denotation bridge, the homomorphism and its tactic, and the seal of the computation* metrology: the provenance hypergraph meets the tape here\n* FusedKinds, LutCarrier, Platform* the kinded fused form; the table fetch as a declared vocabulary extension; host capacity and memory-bounded sizing, every count and byte figure a kinded quantity* metrology applied to the computation itself\n\nThe code generator and TorchLean's certified kernel compiler are complementary, not redundant.\nProgram.of proves a Lean scalar function equal to a kernel expression and the emitted CUDA\nequal to the expression, and its expressions have no transcendental primitives: on the pinned\nrevision it refuses Float.exp, Float.sqrt and min alike. The calculus's code generator\ncovers that alphabet and the table fetch, proves the printed program equal to the tape, and\nleaves each printer's dozen forms and the host's exp and log to trust. A framework that\noffered both would let a branchless model be compiled end to end by one certified path. Which\nside should absorb the other is a design question for the framework's maintainers.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Metrology on a Verified Numerical Framework","header":"19.4. What the calculus built on the framework","id":"/Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework/What-the-calculus-built-on-the-framework/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework--What-the-calculus-built-on-the-framework"},"/The-Function-Calculus-and-Complex-Valued-Carriers-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_/Complex-valued-carriers/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Function-Calculus-and-Complex-Valued-Carriers-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_--Complex-valued-carriers":{"contents":"\n\n is the complexification of a carrier — the functor\n that, given the real arithmetic of , equips\n with the complex arithmetic. A complex-valued quantity is\n; the carrier varies\n( to prove, Float to run) while the kind  does not.This is a fourth point on the representation axis (R10): the three scalar carriers\n( to prove, a binary32 rounding spec to certify, Float to run) are all real,\nand  lifts each of them —  to prove,\n to certify rounding, \nto run. It is a Carrier, and a LawfulCarrier whenever  is (complex addition is\ncomponentwise, so the additivity laws lift for free); it carries a full field \nbut is unordered, so — unlike the real carriers — it sits outside the\nordinal/interval/ratio carrier tower: complex magnitudes are compared by modulus, not ranked.\n\nRealized as Complex R with the toolchain instances (, , , ,\nnegation, zero, one), a Carrier instance, and  / . Why parametric rather\nthan Mathlib's ? For the same reason the carrier is a parameter at all:\n is noncomputable (its parts are , which does not reduce — no\n-valued program runs or compiles, yet the executable carrier must be\nFloat-backed), and it is hardwired to  (there is no \" over\nFloat\"), whereas the representation axis needs complex over Float, over a binary32\nrounding spec, and over  from one definition. As with the reals — where\n is one instance of the carrier, not the universal one\n— Mathlib's  is the natural proof-carrier target: a ring isomorphism\n (its home the Dimension layer) transports\nany property of complex quantities at  to  and borrows its analysis\nlibrary, while the run side stays on .\n\nThe principal complex square root. The algebraic operations are total and\nsingle-valued, so they are plain instances; the square root is the exception — it has a\nbranch cut along the negative real axis. Selecting the principal branch needs exactly one\nsign decision (the sign of the imaginary part, with ),\nisolated as the one-field capability NegTest so the branch-free complex algebra needs\nonly the toolchain.\n\nRealized as Complex.sqrt:\n\nthe real and imaginary magnitudes two real square roots and the\nimaginary sign the one NegTest decision (Float deciding it computably, \nclassically). Complex is deliberately not made a full MathCarrier: a complex\n// suite would drag in a quadrant-aware angle and an unprovable\nlawfulness story the metrology does not need — the one transcendental the electromagnetic\nforward model uses is given directly and honestly as its own branch-tracked constructor.\n\nThe kind-tracked complex square root. The complex analogue of\nQuantity.sqrt, licensed by the same\nPowerKind : the complex square root of a (dimension-one)\nrelative permittivity is a (dimension-one) refractive index — a checked half-power crossing\nof kinds, now over a complex carrier. The complexification leaves the kind layer untouched,\nso this is the only new kind-level constructor the complex numbers require.\n\nRealized as Quantity.csqrt, the smart constructor wrapping the\nprincipal complex square root under the same PowerKind (1/2) certificate as the real\nhalf-power; named csqrt because the complex root is a genuinely distinct operation (it\nhas a branch cut) even though its kind discipline is identical.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009The Function Calculus and Complex-Valued Carriers (R12)","header":"14.2. Complex-valued carriers","id":"/The-Function-Calculus-and-Complex-Valued-Carriers-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_/Complex-valued-carriers/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Function-Calculus-and-Complex-Valued-Carriers-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_--Complex-valued-carriers"},"/The-Proved-Spine/Scale-___-the-operator-based-division-of-_LANGLE_property_RANGLE_-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-12_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Proved-Spine--Scale-___-the-operator-based-division-of-_LANGLE_property_RANGLE_-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-12_RPAR_":{"contents":"\n\nThe four scale types of Dybkær Fig. 12.21, ordered by operator richness:\n, admitting respectively , then\n, then , then .\n\nA four-constructor inductive with a rank : ScaleType → Nat and the induced\n≤. The lemmas le_def, le_refl, le_trans, and le_antisymm establish that\n≤ is the expected linear order.\n\nThe scale order is a linear order: reflexive (le_refl), transitive\n(le_trans), and antisymmetric (le_antisymm) on the scale types.\n\nReflexivity and transitivity are inherited from Nat.le on rank.\nAntisymmetry reduces to Nat.le_antisymm on the ranks followed by a four-by-four\ncase split (cases a <;> cases b <;> simp_all).\n\nOperator-availability monotonicity. A richer scale licenses every operation a\npoorer one does: if  then AllowsOrder, AllowsDifference, and\nAllowsRatio each transport from  to . This is a meta-theorem OWL2's\nreasoner cannot even phrase, since it quantifies over which operations are\ndefined for a kind. Builds on the scale types.\n\nDecide all sixteen ordered pairs: cases a <;> cases b <;> simp_all [AllowsOrder,\nAllowsDifference, AllowsRatio, le_def, rank].\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009The Proved Spine","header":"6.1. Scale — the operator-based division of ⟨property⟩ (Dybkær Ch. 12)","id":"/The-Proved-Spine/Scale-___-the-operator-based-division-of-_LANGLE_property_RANGLE_-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-12_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Proved-Spine--Scale-___-the-operator-based-division-of-_LANGLE_property_RANGLE_-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-12_RPAR_"}});